Binary Heap
===========
    
binary heap is a binary tree,but it is constructed via array rather than
pointer nodes.
this implementation is simpler than pointer nodes,can reduce the performance
cost than the later.

figure :
        idx
        [ 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8 | ... ] <= Array
              |   |   |   |   |   |   |
              |   |   |   |   |   |   +-->root->right->right
              |   |   |   |   |   +--> root->right->left
              |   |   |   |   +--> root->left->right
              |   |   |   +--> root->left->left
              |   |   +--> root->right
              |   +--> root->left
              +--> root
                        root(1)
                       /    \
                      A(2)   B(3)
                     / \    / \
                    C   D  E   F
                   (4) (5) (6) (7)
                   /
               Void Hole(8)
                   

through the figure shown above,we can know
  > root of binary heap start from idx 1.
  > for a node X
      idx of X->left == idx of X * 2
      idx of X->right == idx of X->left + 1

heap order
----------
    
the parent node's key must be less than its childs' keys,if the binary heap
is "minimum binary heap";the parent node's key must be greater than its
childs' keys,if the binary heap is "maximum binary heap".

supported operations
--------------------
        
  Insertion
  DeleteMin    
  DecreaseKey
  IncreaseKey
  Find

void hole
---------
            
in a binary heap,the void hole is the position where next insertion will
attempts to insert new element at there.

percolate up/down
-----------------
            
if new element broken heap order,we must process "percolate up" for the node.
if DeleteMin is performed,the root node will become void hole,we must process
"percolate down" on the node to let it goes to the bottom of binary tree.

routine :
        procedure bh_percolate_up(bh_array: container of bh,
                                  vidx: index of void hole)
          if vidx > sizeof(bh_array) - 1
          then
            return
    
          pidx := vidx / 2
          if pidx == 0 /* root's idx / 2 == 0 */
          then
            return

          org_vkey := bh_array[vidx];

          while pidx > 0
            if org_vkey < bh_array[pidx]
            then
              bh_array[vidx] := bh_array[pidx]
              vidx := pidx
              pidx := vidx / 2
            else
              bh_array[vidx] := org_vkey
              break
    
        procedure bh_percolate_down(bh_array: container of bh,
                                    vidx: index of void hole)
          if vidx > sizeof(bh_array) ||
             !vidx
          then
            /**
             * idx 0 is not a valid index of bh,thus we return
             * it to indicates an error happened.
             */
            return 0

          /**
           * length() - returns the number of valid elements in
           *            the array
           * # [0] also count in length
           * # we need to ensure,the idx less than @length must
           *   be valid and it is not void hole.
           */
          length := bh_array.length()

          while vidx < length
            /**
             * For large data set,we need to checks type overflow
             * that may cause @lidx or @ridx around up to _zero_,
             * idx _zero_ is not a legal index.
             */
            lidx := vidx * 2
            ridx := lidx + 1
    
            /**
             * @lidx and @ridx should less than @length,if they
             * are valid and corresponds to an element in heap.
             * otherwise,void hole been at the bottom of heap.
             *
             * lidx > length --> ridx > length
             */
            if lidx > length
            then
              break

            /**
             * Select the next element to swap with void hole,
             * the minimum between left and right.
             */
            swap_e_idx := bh_array[lidx] > bh_array[ridx] ?
                          ridx : lidx    

            bh_array[vidx] := bh_array[swap_e_idx]

            vidx := swap_e_idx

          return vidx

        ! For insertion,if there been a void hole is found(because the
          routine bh_percolate_down() returned a valid idx),we can just
          make use of the idx.
          Otherwise,the next insertion position has idx
            @next_pos := bh_array.length()
                        #^because idx start from 0.
          We can store the idx of void hole at [0],if its value is 0,
          that means there is no void hole been found.
    
